# IMY 761 Final System Walkthrough — Voice-Over Script

## Introduction

[Show game starting]

This walkthrough demonstrates the final functionality of our 2D platformer, how we addressed feedback from the beta, how the system supports our research questions, and how the data gathering will be conducted.

Our research investigates the effect of **diegetic versus non-diegetic failure feedback on player frustration and perceived competence**.

In the diegetic condition, feedback is communicated through the companion and player-character reactions. In the non-diegetic condition, the same corrective information is communicated through HUD text and visual arrows.

---

# 1. Final Functionality and Beta Feedback

[Show the same obstacle failed in different ways]

One of the main beta issues was that the feedback did not always explain what the player had actually done wrong.

The final system now tracks whether the player jumped, where they jumped from, and when they jumped. It then classifies the failure as, for example, **no jump, too early, or too late**, before selecting feedback.

This means the same obstacle can produce different feedback depending on the player's actual mistake.

[Show diegetic examples]

The diegetic feedback was also made more informative.

If the player does not jump, the companion turns towards them, barks, crouches and demonstrates the jump.

If they jump too early, the companion passes the player's original take-off point, pauses, and then demonstrates the correct take-off position.

So the companion now shows **what needs to change**, rather than simply showing that the obstacle should be jumped.

[Show character reaction]

We also clarified that the physical reaction refers to the **player character**. In the diegetic condition, the character can stumble or recoil after failure, while the non-diegetic condition communicates this information through the interface.

[Show moving platform]

The moving-platform feedback was also corrected. The companion now interacts with the platform's live position, jumps onto it, travels with it, and jumps off correctly.

In the non-diegetic condition, the marker and arrow update with the moving platform to show where the player should stand and where they should move.

[Show HUD feedback]

The non-diegetic messages were also changed from obvious obstacle-based statements to **mistake-specific corrective feedback**, such as:

"You didn't jump."

"Jumped too early — take off later."

Or:

"Jumped too late — take off sooner."

So the overall process is now:

**detect failure → inspect player behaviour → classify the mistake → provide the correction.**

Most importantly, both conditions use the **same detected mistake and corrective information**.

Only the way that information is presented changes.

This ensures that we are comparing diegetic and non-diegetic feedback, rather than comparing detailed feedback with vague feedback.

