# 2. How the System Supports the Research Questions

[Show primary research question]

The system provides the controlled environment needed to investigate our primary research question.

Both conditions use the same level, hazards, difficulty, failure logic, timing and underlying corrective information.

The main variable that changes is the **feedback modality**.

The first two sub-questions are literature-based, so the system does not answer them directly. Instead, it operationalises the distinction established in the literature: the companion and character reactions represent diegetic feedback, while HUD text and arrows represent non-diegetic feedback.

The third sub-question compares **self-reported frustration and perceived competence** between the two conditions. The game creates the two controlled experiences that participants will later rate using the questionnaire.

The fourth sub-question examines how players describe failure, competence and motivation to continue. This will be explored through the interviews.

The companion also remains present in both conditions so that its presence does not become an additional experimental variable. Only its role in delivering feedback changes.

---

# 3. Data Gathering

[Show two-group diagram]

We plan to recruit approximately **10 to 15 participants** through convenience sampling.

The study uses a **between-subjects design**, meaning each participant experiences only one condition.

Participants will be divided as evenly as possible between the diegetic and non-diegetic groups, giving approximately **five to eight participants per group**.

Gaming experience may be considered when balancing the groups, but it is not being investigated as a separate research question.

Each session should last approximately **25 to 30 minutes**.

[Show session sequence]

Participants will first receive the Participant Information Sheet and sign the Informed Consent Form.

A brief indication of gaming experience may then be collected if needed for group balancing.

The participant will play their assigned condition for up to approximately **15 minutes**.

The game is designed to be challenging enough for participants to encounter the feedback system several times, but failures are **not artificially triggered**.

---

## Post-Play Data

[Show questionnaire]

After gameplay, participants will complete a post-play questionnaire.

For **perceived competence**, we will use relevant items from the **Basic Needs in Games Scale, or BANGS**, measuring competence satisfaction and competence frustration on a seven-point scale.

For **frustration**, we will use relevant **Game Experience Questionnaire, or GEQ**, tension and annoyance items, measuring feelings such as annoyance, irritability and frustration.

[Show interview questions]

Participants will then complete an approximately **five-minute semi-structured interview** exploring their experience of failure, the helpfulness of the feedback, their sense of competence, frustration, and motivation to continue.

With consent, the interviews will be audio-recorded, transcribed and anonymised.

---

## Data Analysis

We will therefore collect both **quantitative and qualitative data**.

The questionnaire results will be summarised using descriptive statistics and compared between the two conditions using **exploratory independent-samples t-tests**.

Because the sample is small, these results will be interpreted cautiously.

The interviews will be analysed using **Braun and Clarke's six-phase reflexive thematic analysis**, focusing on themes around frustration, competence, feedback usefulness and motivation to continue.

---

# Conclusion

[Show both conditions]

Overall, the main improvement from the beta is that the system now responds to **what the player actually did wrong**, rather than simply reacting to the obstacle.

Both conditions share the same failure-detection and corrective logic, while only the presentation changes.

This gives us a more useful feedback system and a more controlled way of investigating the effect of diegetic versus non-diegetic failure feedback on frustration and perceived competence.
